  "narrative": "During the 2020s, Black and African women's hairstyles and hair culture continued to thrive, building on the momentum of previous decades. The natural hair movement, which originated in the 1960s and gained popularity in the 2000s, remained a driving force, encouraging individuals to celebrate their kinky hair textures. Styles like afros, cornrows, and box braids were (and continue to be) deeply rooted in African cultures and traditions, symbolizing identity, community, and resistance against Eurocentric beauty standards. Protective hairstyles like Bantu knots and locs became increasingly popular as women sought to minimize hair manipulation and exposure to environmental elements that can damage Afro-textured hair. While documentation from the 2020s specifically is limited within this source material, it is clear that Black and African women's hair culture remains a vital aspect of their identity, creativity, and self-expression.",
